WEBVTT

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:04.509
Toute votre vie, cette
aiguille a indiqué le nord.

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:08.105
Mais les fonds marins ont
la mémoire plus longue.

00:00:08.185 --> 00:00:10.233
Nord et sud se sont inversés.

00:00:10.313 --> 00:00:13.279
Encore et encore, sans aucun calendrier.

00:00:14.153 --> 00:00:18.467
Cette aiguille suit un champ
qui enveloppe toute la planète.

00:00:18.547 --> 00:00:20.298
On le croirait permanent.

00:00:20.378 --> 00:00:21.580
Il ne l'est pas.

00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:26.568
Aux dorsales océaniques, la lave
fraîche refroidit et se fige en roche.

00:00:26.648 --> 00:00:30.704
De minuscules grains magnétiques y
enregistrent la direction du champ.

00:00:30.784 --> 00:00:35.253
Les fonds marins s'écartent, et chaque
renversement ouvre une nouvelle bande.

00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:38.362
Cinq millions d'années,
une vingtaine de renversements,

00:00:38.442 --> 00:00:40.976
et des intervalles extrêmement inégaux.

00:00:42.053 --> 00:00:43.310
Alors, deux questions.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.863
Quand aura lieu le prochain renversement ?

00:00:45.943 --> 00:00:48.841
Et à quelle fréquence le
champ se renverse-t-il ?

00:00:48.921 --> 00:00:50.713
À laquelle peut-on répondre ?

00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:57.044
Dans un modèle numérique simple
d'un champ qui se renverse,

00:00:57.124 --> 00:00:59.262
la première est sans espoir.

00:00:59.342 --> 00:01:00.542
La seconde, non.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:06.019
Pour comprendre pourquoi, nous passerons
par le métal liquide et le chaos.

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:10.738
Puis par des machines qui imaginent
des instantanés de champs magnétiques.

00:01:10.818 --> 00:01:16.205
Elles doivent obéir exactement à certaines
lois, et à d'autres seulement en moyenne.

00:01:17.587 --> 00:01:20.363
À quelle fréquence le
champ se renverse-t-il ?

00:01:20.443 --> 00:01:22.862
Une carte pour s'initier au projet MilaDy,

00:01:22.942 --> 00:01:26.247
de la boussole jusqu'à la
recherche d'aujourd'hui.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.219
Les premières simulations
doivent-elles viser

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:33.334
la fréquence des renversements du champ,

00:01:33.414 --> 00:01:36.758
ou les signes annonciateurs
juste avant un renversement ?

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.920
Y a-t-il un aimant géant caché
à l'intérieur de la Terre ?

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.070
Tout au fond, le noyau externe
est un océan de fer liquide.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:55.979
Livré à lui-même, un champ s'y éteindrait
en quelques dizaines de milliers d'années.

00:01:56.059 --> 00:02:01.185
Pourtant, le champ de la Terre existe
depuis plusieurs milliards d'années.

00:02:01.265 --> 00:02:05.375
Le fer en mouvement doit donc
le fabriquer, en permanence.

00:02:06.553 --> 00:02:11.354
Prenez un barreau aimanté :
le nord à un bout, le sud à l'autre.

00:02:11.434 --> 00:02:12.726
Coupez-le en deux.

00:02:12.806 --> 00:02:17.302
Vous obtenez deux aimants,
qui ont chacun un nord et un sud.

00:02:17.382 --> 00:02:21.378
Personne n'a jamais trouvé
de pôle magnétique isolé.

00:02:22.248 --> 00:02:27.473
Les lignes de champ disent la même
chose : elles n'ont ni début ni fin.

00:02:27.553 --> 00:02:31.134
Tracez une boîte,
n'importe laquelle, et comptez.

00:02:31.214 --> 00:02:34.123
Il entre autant de lignes qu'il en sort.

00:02:34.203 --> 00:02:36.250
Gardez cette boîte en tête.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:43.043
Une dynamo de vélo transforme le mouvement
en électricité, grâce à un aimant intégré.

00:02:43.123 --> 00:02:47.313
La Terre, elle, doit fabriquer
le sien. L'astuce : une boucle.

00:02:48.183 --> 00:02:52.534
Approchez un micro du haut-parleur
qui le diffuse, et montez le gain.

00:02:52.614 --> 00:02:55.808
En dessous d'un certain point,
un murmure s'éteint.

00:02:55.888 --> 00:02:58.487
Au-delà, la boucle s'alimente elle-même.

00:03:01.357 --> 00:03:04.589
Faites maintenant tourner
un disque de cuivre,

00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:08.200
relié à une bobine
enroulée autour de son axe.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.219
Le courant crée le champ,
et le champ crée le courant.

00:03:12.299 --> 00:03:16.497
Faites-le tourner de plus en
plus vite. Le champ grandit-il

00:03:16.577 --> 00:03:21.113
petit à petit, ou pas du tout
avant une certaine vitesse ?

00:03:24.983 --> 00:03:26.868
Pas du tout, jusqu'à un seuil.

00:03:26.948 --> 00:03:30.355
Au-delà, chaque tour multiplie
un minuscule champ germe

00:03:30.435 --> 00:03:34.268
par le même facteur : c'est
une croissance exponentielle.

00:03:34.348 --> 00:03:37.543
Juste au-dessus du seuil,
la croissance est lente.

00:03:37.623 --> 00:03:41.451
Puis le champ freine le disque,
et la croissance s'arrête.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.093
Qui paie la facture ?

00:03:44.173 --> 00:03:48.537
Un moteur entraîne l'écoulement ;
dans la Terre, c'est la convection.

00:03:48.617 --> 00:03:52.338
L'écoulement étire le champ ;
le champ freine l'écoulement.

00:03:52.418 --> 00:03:55.249
Une fois la croissance
arrêtée, chaque watt qui

00:03:55.329 --> 00:03:58.521
entre ressort sous forme
de chaleur, en moyenne.

00:03:58.601 --> 00:04:03.398
Une dynamo transforme le mouvement en
champ ; elle ne crée aucune énergie.

00:04:04.553 --> 00:04:09.925
Le champ de la Terre pointe aujourd'hui
dans un sens. Lequel devrait-il préférer ?

00:04:13.295 --> 00:04:18.884
Inversez chaque ligne de champ : les
équations de la dynamo restent les mêmes.

00:04:18.964 --> 00:04:24.964
Donc, aucun des deux. Ce sont des jumeaux
en miroir, tout aussi permis par les lois.

00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:28.016
Le sens qu'on obtient
relève de l'histoire,

00:04:28.096 --> 00:04:32.257
pas des lois : dans le disque,
le signe du germe décide.

00:04:32.337 --> 00:04:35.928
Et rien dans les lois
n'interdit un renversement.

00:04:37.487 --> 00:04:42.535
Du fer en mouvement, une boucle avec
un seuil, aucun sens privilégié.

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:46.995
Mais le disque avait des fils ;
le fer liquide n'en a aucun.

00:04:47.075 --> 00:04:51.490
Alors, quel mouvement en est
capable, et à quelle vitesse ?

00:04:52.953 --> 00:04:56.264
Près du seuil, les simulations
de dynamo doivent-elles

00:04:56.344 --> 00:05:00.380
partir d'un germe minuscule,
d'un germe fort, ou des deux ?

00:05:05.953 --> 00:05:09.566
Quel mouvement peut faire
croître un champ magnétique ?

00:05:09.646 --> 00:05:11.305
Dans un conducteur parfait,

00:05:11.385 --> 00:05:15.406
les lignes de champ seraient
des fils gelés dans le fluide.

00:05:15.486 --> 00:05:19.545
Partout où l'écoulement étire
un fil, le champ s'y renforce.

00:05:20.415 --> 00:05:25.291
Dans un vrai métal, les fils s'estompent,
comme de l'encre qui s'étale.

00:05:25.371 --> 00:05:30.667
La vitesse de cet estompage est fixée
par la diffusivité magnétique du métal.

00:05:30.747 --> 00:05:32.691
Même écoulement, deux métaux.

00:05:32.771 --> 00:05:37.508
Dans un bon conducteur, l'estompage
est lent : les fils restent nets.

00:05:37.588 --> 00:05:42.225
Dans un mauvais, il est rapide :
les fils se fondent dans le flou.

00:05:43.387 --> 00:05:47.912
Un seul nombre compte les points :
le nombre de Reynolds magnétique, Rm.

00:05:47.992 --> 00:05:49.943
C'est la vitesse de l'écoulement,

00:05:50.023 --> 00:05:54.181
multipliée par sa taille, divisée
par la diffusivité magnétique.

00:05:54.261 --> 00:05:57.571
À grand Rm, l'étirement va
plus vite que l'estompage.

00:05:57.651 --> 00:05:59.855
À petit Rm, l'estompage l'emporte.

00:06:00.725 --> 00:06:05.733
Tournez le bouton pour augmenter Rm,
et observez un faible champ germe.

00:06:05.813 --> 00:06:07.882
À petit Rm, le germe s'éteint.

00:06:07.962 --> 00:06:11.757
Au-delà d'un seuil, le taux
de croissance franchit zéro,

00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:14.728
et le germe grandit, comme le larsen.

00:06:15.887 --> 00:06:19.645
Les dynamos de laboratoire
utilisent du sodium liquide.

00:06:19.725 --> 00:06:22.157
Il coule aussi facilement que l'eau.

00:06:22.237 --> 00:06:27.740
Mais un champ s'y estompe environ cent
mille fois plus vite que ses tourbillons.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:31.173
Le rapport de ces deux
vitesses, un sur cent mille,

00:06:31.253 --> 00:06:34.410
c'est le nombre de Prandtl magnétique, Pm.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.792
Pour atteindre un Rm de quelques
dizaines, il faut brasser violemment.

00:06:39.872 --> 00:06:42.190
Le nombre de Reynolds de l'écoulement,

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:46.024
la jauge de la turbulence,
se chiffre alors en millions.

00:06:46.104 --> 00:06:49.283
Toute dynamo de laboratoire
est donc turbulente.

00:06:50.453 --> 00:06:53.774
Seules trois expériences au
sodium liquide ont fabriqué

00:06:53.854 --> 00:06:57.290
leur propre champ, chaque
fois d'un genre différent.

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:59.995
À Riga, un écoulement
en tire-bouchon a fait

00:07:00.075 --> 00:07:03.246
naître un champ qui oscille
et tourne lentement.

00:07:03.326 --> 00:07:06.393
À Karlsruhe, cinquante-deux
cellules en spirale ont

00:07:06.473 --> 00:07:09.779
produit un champ stationnaire,
proche d'un dipôle.

00:07:09.859 --> 00:07:15.141
Et seule l'expérience VKS, à Cadarache,
a donné un dipôle capable de se renverser.

00:07:16.011 --> 00:07:19.485
Une quatrième, DRESDYN, à Dresde,
cherche à les rejoindre :

00:07:19.565 --> 00:07:23.027
un cylindre en rotation
dont l'axe tourne lentement.

00:07:24.187 --> 00:07:27.104
À l'intérieur de VKS :
un cylindre qui contient

00:07:27.184 --> 00:07:29.954
cent cinquante litres de sodium liquide.

00:07:30.034 --> 00:07:35.313
À ses extrémités, deux disques à pales,
les turbines, tournent en sens opposés.

00:07:36.183 --> 00:07:39.501
Le métal des disques devrait-il
avoir de l'importance ?

00:07:42.871 --> 00:07:45.500
Avec des disques en acier inoxydable,

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:49.388
il n'y a eu aucune dynamo,
même à pleine vitesse.

00:07:49.468 --> 00:07:55.468
Remplacez-les par des disques en fer doux,
et un dipôle grandit le long de l'axe.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:02.883
Le seuil ne dépend donc pas que de
Rm : les matériaux comptent aussi.

00:08:04.053 --> 00:08:08.541
Faites maintenant tourner les deux
disques à des vitesses différentes.

00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:11.353
Pour certains réglages,
le dipôle se renverse

00:08:11.433 --> 00:08:14.153
encore et encore, sans aucun calendrier.

00:08:14.233 --> 00:08:19.288
Comme l'aiguille de la boussole du début,
mais toutes les une à deux minutes.

00:08:20.158 --> 00:08:23.693
Chaque renversement, lui,
ne dure qu'environ cinq secondes.

00:08:23.773 --> 00:08:26.696
Des bandes inégales,
comme celles des fonds marins,

00:08:26.776 --> 00:08:30.051
mais écrites en minutes,
pas en millions d'années.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:35.405
Impossible de simuler le tout petit
Pm du sodium : quelles valeurs

00:08:35.485 --> 00:08:39.980
de Pm couvrir dans les premières
simulations en trois dimensions ?

00:08:45.553 --> 00:08:50.656
Riga, Karlsruhe et VKS ont couronné
une traque de près d'un siècle.

00:08:50.736 --> 00:08:52.158
En voici la carte.

00:08:53.028 --> 00:08:55.586
Première piste, les archives de la Terre :

00:08:55.666 --> 00:08:59.153
des laves aimantées à l'envers,
mille neuf cent six.

00:08:59.233 --> 00:09:03.071
Les bandes des fonds marins,
mille neuf cent soixante-trois.

00:09:03.151 --> 00:09:06.789
Puis les renversements vus
comme des événements aléatoires,

00:09:06.869 --> 00:09:09.908
dont la probabilité
change au fil des âges.

00:09:11.087 --> 00:09:12.647
Deuxième piste, la théorie.

00:09:12.727 --> 00:09:15.564
Mille neuf cent dix-neuf :
un fluide conducteur en

00:09:15.644 --> 00:09:18.722
mouvement pourrait entretenir
le champ du Soleil.

00:09:18.802 --> 00:09:21.643
Mille neuf cent trente-trois :
aucun écoulement ne

00:09:21.723 --> 00:09:25.090
peut entretenir un champ
symétrique autour d'un axe.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:27.646
Mille neuf cent cinquante-huit :

00:09:27.726 --> 00:09:31.644
les premiers exemples démontrés
d'écoulements qui en sont capables.

00:09:31.724 --> 00:09:35.538
Dans les années soixante-dix arrivent
des écoulements dynamos simples,

00:09:35.618 --> 00:09:38.729
qui deviendront les plans
de Riga et de Karlsruhe.

00:09:39.887 --> 00:09:42.248
Troisième piste : construire une dynamo.

00:09:42.328 --> 00:09:44.111
Mille neuf cent soixante-trois :

00:09:44.191 --> 00:09:47.556
une maquette de dynamo à
cylindres de fer en rotation.

00:09:47.636 --> 00:09:49.843
Mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-dix-neuf :

00:09:49.923 --> 00:09:53.890
le sodium fabrique son propre
champ, à Riga, puis à Karlsruhe.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:57.750
Deux mille six : dans VKS,
l'effet dynamo n'apparaît

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:00.998
qu'avec des disques en fer ;
puis viennent les premiers

00:10:01.078 --> 00:10:04.242
renversements d'une dynamo
turbulente au sodium.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.596
Quatrième piste, les ordinateurs.

00:10:07.676 --> 00:10:09.351
Mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-un :

00:10:09.431 --> 00:10:12.151
une turbulence simulée
fait naître des dynamos.

00:10:12.231 --> 00:10:14.131
Mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-quinze :

00:10:14.211 --> 00:10:17.268
dans un noyau terrestre simulé,
le champ se renverse.

00:10:17.348 --> 00:10:21.824
Les repères cerclés signalent les articles
cosignés par les encadrants du projet.

00:10:21.904 --> 00:10:24.744
Mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-dix-sept :
en simulation,

00:10:24.824 --> 00:10:28.235
deux tourbillons contrarotatifs
produisent un effet dynamo.

00:10:28.315 --> 00:10:32.562
Deux mille cinq : ces dynamos fonctionnent
encore pour un Pm d'un centième.

00:10:33.432 --> 00:10:35.173
À partir de deux mille seize,

00:10:35.253 --> 00:10:39.365
deux codes simulent la cuve de
VKS, turbines en fer comprises.

00:10:39.445 --> 00:10:43.372
Deux mille vingt-six : tous deux
obtiennent des renversements ;

00:10:43.452 --> 00:10:48.187
dans les cas explorés, seulement avec
un milieu conducteur tout autour.

00:10:48.267 --> 00:10:50.076
Leur simulation de référence :

00:10:50.156 --> 00:10:54.832
un nombre de Reynolds d'environ
mille six cents, et non des millions.

00:10:55.987 --> 00:10:59.355
Cinquième piste : quelques
cadrans au lieu d'une cuve.

00:10:59.435 --> 00:11:01.309
Mille neuf cent cinquante-huit :

00:11:01.389 --> 00:11:04.783
deux dynamos à disque,
couplées, se renversent déjà.

00:11:04.863 --> 00:11:08.178
Deux mille douze : trois
cadrans qui se renversent sous

00:11:08.258 --> 00:11:12.044
le seul effet du chaos ;
nous jouerons bientôt avec eux.

00:11:12.914 --> 00:11:15.850
Deux mille vingt-six :
quatre cadrans ajustés sur

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:17.882
les renversements simulés de VKS,

00:11:17.962 --> 00:11:21.985
par une méthode qui découvre
les équations dans les données.

00:11:23.153 --> 00:11:27.394
MilaDy, lancé en deux mille vingt-six,
apporte l'apprentissage automatique

00:11:27.474 --> 00:11:31.372
là où se rencontrent les cuves,
les ordinateurs et les cadrans.

00:11:31.452 --> 00:11:35.515
Pourtant, la piste des données reste
mince : en deux mille vingt-deux,

00:11:35.595 --> 00:11:38.889
les méthodes d'apprentissage
essayées par une étude n'ont

00:11:38.969 --> 00:11:42.909
pas su prévoir de façon fiable
un renversement géomagnétique.

00:11:43.779 --> 00:11:47.509
Le stage et un projet de
thèse y ajoutent un pari :

00:11:47.589 --> 00:11:53.589
des machines qui imaginent des champs
de dynamo et obéissent à leur physique.

00:11:54.542 --> 00:11:58.271
Derrière chaque piste, la même course :
l'étirement contre l'estompage.

00:12:02.653 --> 00:12:07.881
Back to the race: the flow stretches
the field, and diffusion smudges it.

00:12:07.961 --> 00:12:12.135
Physicists write the very same
race as a single equation.

00:12:13.005 --> 00:12:17.513
On the left: how fast the magnetic
field changes, at each point.

00:12:17.593 --> 00:12:20.628
First term on the right:
the flow carries the

00:12:20.708 --> 00:12:24.181
field along and stretches
it, like the threads.

00:12:24.261 --> 00:12:29.701
Second term: diffusion smudges it,
at a pace set by the magnetic diffusivity.

00:12:29.781 --> 00:12:31.852
A poor conductor smudges fast.

00:12:31.932 --> 00:12:36.049
Stretching against smudging:
the whole race, in one line.

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:42.297
Picture the flow as a conveyor
belt that does not feel its cargo.

00:12:42.377 --> 00:12:48.089
A weak field rides on the imposed flow,
and fades or grows exponentially.

00:12:48.169 --> 00:12:53.277
On a log scale: a straight line,
whose slope is the growth rate.

00:12:54.147 --> 00:12:57.326
But a strong field
pushes back on the flow,

00:12:57.406 --> 00:13:02.173
as it braked the copper disc:
the line bends into a plateau.

00:13:02.253 --> 00:13:05.603
These are the kinematic
and dynamic regimes.

00:13:05.683 --> 00:13:07.739
Flips live on the plateau.

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:12.824
To test ideas, physicists
need a flow simple enough

00:13:12.904 --> 00:13:16.874
to write on one line,
yet able to make a dynamo.

00:13:16.954 --> 00:13:19.635
One classic choice: the ABC flow.

00:13:20.505 --> 00:13:23.526
It fills space with
identical twisting rooms.

00:13:23.606 --> 00:13:26.502
Three crossed winds make
the fluid corkscrew:

00:13:26.582 --> 00:13:29.382
it spins around the direction it moves.

00:13:29.462 --> 00:13:32.042
This box returns higher on our climb.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:38.377
Now turn one knob on this flow: Rm.
Turning it up means less diffusion.

00:13:38.457 --> 00:13:41.880
Less smudging should
only help the stretching.

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:46.890
So once the dynamo switches
on, it stays on. Right?

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:54.642
Here is the growth rate,
computed at twenty-four settings.

00:13:54.722 --> 00:14:00.244
Below about nine, every field fades. Then
growth turns positive: the dynamo is on.

00:14:00.324 --> 00:14:03.592
Near seventeen and a half,
it switches off again.

00:14:03.672 --> 00:14:06.325
Above about twenty-seven, it comes back.

00:14:06.405 --> 00:14:11.517
Not one threshold, but windows: turning
a knob further does not always help.

00:14:11.597 --> 00:14:14.403
It agrees with published
results. No machine

00:14:14.483 --> 00:14:17.502
learning yet: this only tests the solver.

00:14:18.887 --> 00:14:22.238
Real dynamos have not
one knob, but a panel.

00:14:22.318 --> 00:14:26.292
How hard the metal is stirred;
how well it conducts.

00:14:26.372 --> 00:14:29.879
What the discs are made
of: remember the iron.

00:14:29.959 --> 00:14:34.167
How unequal their speeds are:
that is when VKS flipped.

00:14:34.247 --> 00:14:38.643
For DRESDYN, a precessing tank:
how fast its axis turns.

00:14:39.513 --> 00:14:43.064
Even the wall counts: in VKS
simulations, changing only its

00:14:43.144 --> 00:14:47.351
magnetic condition raised the
threshold by about forty percent.

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:53.427
Every turbulent dynamo simulation
in three dimensions is very costly.

00:14:53.507 --> 00:14:56.969
So only a handful of knob
settings get simulated.

00:14:57.049 --> 00:15:02.934
Each one is a mountain of data: a single
snapshot can weigh more than a gigabyte.

00:15:03.804 --> 00:15:06.421
Each run holds only a few flips.

00:15:06.501 --> 00:15:10.409
And between the dots,
there is nothing at all.

00:15:11.587 --> 00:15:14.288
Those gaps are where this film lives.

00:15:14.368 --> 00:15:17.781
The study behind this
film asks three things.

00:15:18.651 --> 00:15:22.541
One: can a machine learn
the whole family of fields,

00:15:22.621 --> 00:15:25.818
even at knob settings between the dots?

00:15:25.898 --> 00:15:29.186
Two: can it respect
each law where it holds,

00:15:29.266 --> 00:15:33.695
exactly in every field,
or on average over many fields?

00:15:33.775 --> 00:15:37.585
Three: can it tell us how
often the field flips?

00:15:38.455 --> 00:15:41.785
To find out, the study's
plan climbs a staircase,

00:15:41.865 --> 00:15:45.883
from three numbers to the
shapes of real experiments.

00:15:46.753 --> 00:15:50.114
One honest note: this
independent study is prospective,

00:15:50.194 --> 00:15:53.304
built on small experiments
on an ordinary computer,

00:15:53.384 --> 00:15:57.468
and not endorsed by MilaDy,
its partners or the supervisors.

00:15:59.253 --> 00:16:03.448
An equation, windows, a panel
of knobs, a handful of dots.

00:16:03.528 --> 00:16:05.656
Full simulations cost too much,

00:16:05.736 --> 00:16:10.229
so we start with a three-dial
caricature that already flips.

00:16:10.309 --> 00:16:12.845
Can anyone say when it flips next?

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:17.583
With only a handful of runs,
should the settings

00:16:17.663 --> 00:16:21.336
be spread evenly,
or packed near each threshold?

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:30.801
Here is the caricature:
three dials, linked by springs.

00:16:30.881 --> 00:16:34.810
One is the dipole,
the north-south part of the field.

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:37.836
One is the quadrupole, a four-pole part.

00:16:37.916 --> 00:16:41.955
The third is a flow pattern,
driven by a knob, Gamma.

00:16:42.825 --> 00:16:46.537
Three short equations, from
Gissinger, tie them together.

00:16:46.617 --> 00:16:50.195
Flip both field dials,
and the equations read the same.

00:16:50.275 --> 00:16:54.362
Three dials, three numbers:
the bottom step of the staircase.

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:59.278
Use two dials as a map:
quadrupole across, dipole up.

00:16:59.358 --> 00:17:03.985
With the knob low, the state
circles in one whirlpool, and stays.

00:17:04.065 --> 00:17:08.040
A mirror twin circles the
mirror whirlpool: dipole down.

00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:11.988
Each start is trapped.
The dipole never changes sign.

00:17:12.858 --> 00:17:14.076
Now turn Gamma up.

00:17:14.156 --> 00:17:19.772
Just above zero point eight six, the
whirlpools touch: a narrow corridor opens.

00:17:19.852 --> 00:17:24.675
The state lingers on one side,
then slips through. The dipole flips.

00:17:24.755 --> 00:17:29.327
Nothing here is random. So,
will the flips come like clockwork?

00:17:32.697 --> 00:17:35.389
No. Long waits and
short ones, irregularly.

00:17:35.469 --> 00:17:39.302
The dipole's sign prints uneven
stripes, like the seafloor.

00:17:40.487 --> 00:17:44.634
Now count: one tick each
time the dipole crosses zero.

00:17:44.714 --> 00:17:50.270
Not every tick is a flip: an excursion
can cross and come straight back.

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:53.668
Ticks per unit of time: the crossing rate.

00:17:54.538 --> 00:17:57.202
Up to zero point eight six: silence.

00:17:57.282 --> 00:18:01.166
Just above, about two ticks
per thousand time units.

00:18:01.246 --> 00:18:04.020
At Gamma equal to one, about eleven.

00:18:05.187 --> 00:18:10.946
Set Gamma at zero point nine, and
launch two copies, one millionth apart.

00:18:11.026 --> 00:18:17.026
We know the state to six decimals.
Can we predict the date of the next flip?

00:18:21.005 --> 00:18:23.279
At first, the copies move as one.

00:18:23.359 --> 00:18:27.061
But their gap nearly triples
every eleven time units.

00:18:27.141 --> 00:18:31.309
After about a hundred and fifty,
they disagree completely.

00:18:32.179 --> 00:18:36.246
The average wait between crossings
is longer: over two hundred.

00:18:36.326 --> 00:18:40.394
Each extra decimal buys only
about twenty-five more time units.

00:18:40.474 --> 00:18:45.770
So the forecast fades before the average
wait ends. Only the statistics survive.

00:18:46.953 --> 00:18:50.376
The path is lost,
so leave the shutter open.

00:18:50.456 --> 00:18:54.117
Each pass leaves a little
light: a glow builds.

00:18:54.197 --> 00:18:59.133
A copy started on the other side
first paints the mirror image.

00:18:59.213 --> 00:19:02.188
Given time, both paint the same glow.

00:19:03.058 --> 00:19:07.464
Where does the dipole spend its
time? Two humps, the same for both.

00:19:07.544 --> 00:19:11.080
This reproducible glow is
the stationary distribution.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:15.006
Below the threshold, each
start paints its own whirlpool.

00:19:16.187 --> 00:19:20.235
Now film the trajectory,
one frame every two time units.

00:19:20.315 --> 00:19:22.520
Neighbouring frames look alike.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:24.363
Cut the film into frames.

00:19:24.443 --> 00:19:28.860
Drop them in a shoebox, shake,
and throw away the timestamps.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:32.655
Pour the box out:
the frames redraw the same glow.

00:19:33.525 --> 00:19:37.464
Each frame is a snapshot:
three dial readings, nothing more.

00:19:37.544 --> 00:19:40.278
It carries no timestamp and no time order.

00:19:40.358 --> 00:19:44.297
Yet the shoebox, plus the equations,
answers many questions.

00:19:44.377 --> 00:19:48.761
Averages, energy budgets, even
how often the dipole crosses zero.

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:53.999
Pick one photo. Its three
dial readings give its energy.

00:19:54.079 --> 00:19:59.198
From that photo alone, the equations
tell how fast its energy changes.

00:19:59.278 --> 00:20:02.279
Drop one marble per photo at that value.

00:20:03.149 --> 00:20:08.315
Some photos are gaining energy,
others losing it. The pile spreads wide.

00:20:08.395 --> 00:20:10.428
Yet its average sits at zero.

00:20:10.508 --> 00:20:13.453
Energy in equals energy out, on average.

00:20:14.323 --> 00:20:17.059
Like a bank account: over the years,

00:20:17.139 --> 00:20:21.504
income matches spending,
yet each day gains or loses.

00:20:21.584 --> 00:20:27.503
This is a law of the crowd. It holds
for the shoebox, not for each photo.

00:20:28.787 --> 00:20:32.431
How many independent snapshots
does each setting need,

00:20:32.511 --> 00:20:35.392
and how far apart must they be saved?

00:20:40.753 --> 00:20:44.515
A puzzle. How many cars
cross this line each hour?

00:20:44.595 --> 00:20:50.235
No clock. Just one photo from above,
where every speedometer can be read.

00:20:51.105 --> 00:20:56.067
Can one photo, with no time
in it, give you a rate?

00:20:59.437 --> 00:21:02.721
It can. Draw a thin strip around the line.

00:21:02.801 --> 00:21:08.801
A fast car spends little time inside,
so catching one there counts for more.

00:21:09.789 --> 00:21:15.510
Add up the speeds of the cars inside,
and divide by the strip's width.

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:19.682
On average, that is how
many cars cross per hour.

00:21:20.853 --> 00:21:26.190
Back to the shoebox. Spread its
photos out by their dipole reading.

00:21:26.270 --> 00:21:30.746
The line to cross is zero:
draw a thin strip around it.

00:21:31.616 --> 00:21:34.172
Each photo also needs a speedometer.

00:21:34.252 --> 00:21:37.425
No clock required:
the dipole's equation turns

00:21:37.505 --> 00:21:40.908
the photo's three dial
readings into a speed.

00:21:40.988 --> 00:21:44.386
Up or down, both count,
so keep only its size.

00:21:45.256 --> 00:21:47.492
Add the speeds inside the strip,

00:21:47.572 --> 00:21:52.061
then divide by its width and
by the total number of photos.

00:21:52.141 --> 00:21:54.157
Out comes the crossing rate.

00:21:54.237 --> 00:21:57.706
Shrink the strip to nothing,
and for well-behaved

00:21:57.786 --> 00:22:01.192
systems the recipe is
exact: Rice's formula.

00:22:01.272 --> 00:22:04.732
The catch: few photos land
in a thin strip. Here,

00:22:04.812 --> 00:22:07.888
about two dozen out of eight thousand.

00:22:09.053 --> 00:22:10.982
Now test it against the truth.

00:22:11.062 --> 00:22:12.970
At forty-one settings of Gamma,

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:16.606
long simulations count the
crossings as they happen.

00:22:16.686 --> 00:22:21.001
Rice gets only the true snapshots,
shuffled, plus the equations.

00:22:21.871 --> 00:22:24.006
The two curves almost coincide.

00:22:24.086 --> 00:22:26.961
Shade the gap,
and divide by the area under

00:22:27.041 --> 00:22:30.010
the counted curve: the cumulative error.

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:33.869
Four percent near the threshold,
six percent above it.

00:22:33.949 --> 00:22:36.618
A good sign, on three chaotic dials that

00:22:36.698 --> 00:22:39.983
are not even guaranteed
to be well-behaved.

00:22:41.553 --> 00:22:44.964
One caution: Rice counts
crossings, not reversals.

00:22:45.044 --> 00:22:49.852
An excursion across zero and back
adds two crossings, but no reversal.

00:22:49.932 --> 00:22:52.604
To isolate reversals, the PhD plan counts

00:22:52.684 --> 00:22:55.967
crossings of a far level,
on the other side.

00:22:57.353 --> 00:23:00.628
So Rice needs only
snapshots and the equations,

00:23:00.708 --> 00:23:03.783
but plenty of snapshots, and cheap ones.

00:23:03.863 --> 00:23:08.369
Yet real dynamo simulations exist
at only a few knob settings.

00:23:08.449 --> 00:23:11.698
Between the dots,
there is no shoebox at all.

00:23:11.778 --> 00:23:15.876
How do you get snapshots at
a setting nobody simulated?

00:23:17.553 --> 00:23:20.201
For VKS, which dipole should Rice count,

00:23:20.281 --> 00:23:24.158
and what threshold turns a
crossing into a reversal?

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:34.342
New snapshots start as pure noise:
a round cloud of random points.

00:23:34.422 --> 00:23:38.445
Over it, the crowd we want:
one point per snapshot. Here,

00:23:38.525 --> 00:23:41.879
two mirror blobs, like the two whirlpools.

00:23:42.749 --> 00:23:45.694
Now let a wind blow through the cloud.

00:23:45.774 --> 00:23:50.870
Each point drifts, bends,
and lands inside the shape of the data.

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:55.518
Fresh noise in, fresh snapshots
out, as many as you like.

00:23:56.388 --> 00:24:01.145
That wind is a generative model:
new, plausible examples, on demand.

00:24:01.225 --> 00:24:03.816
The whole game is learning the wind.

00:24:04.987 --> 00:24:07.420
One warning before the formulas.

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:12.053
The wind runs on its own
transport clock, from zero to one.

00:24:12.133 --> 00:24:17.346
Not physical time: at the end,
you hold one snapshot, not a movie.

00:24:18.216 --> 00:24:23.999
And the wind's arrows move snapshots, not
fluid: they are not the fluid's velocity.

00:24:25.153 --> 00:24:28.306
How is the wind learned?
With a simple trick.

00:24:28.386 --> 00:24:34.052
Keep one coordinate of each point, and
lay the transport clock out to the right.

00:24:34.132 --> 00:24:37.153
Pair each noise point
with a random snapshot;

00:24:37.233 --> 00:24:39.727
join them with a straight string.

00:24:39.807 --> 00:24:42.854
Thousands of strings, crossing everywhere.

00:24:43.724 --> 00:24:45.554
Pick one place, at one moment.

00:24:45.634 --> 00:24:48.990
Several strings pass through,
pointing different ways.

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:52.809
The network learns one arrow
there: their average direction.

00:24:52.889 --> 00:24:55.542
That is the whole training:
plain regression,

00:24:55.622 --> 00:24:58.138
like fitting a curve through points.

00:24:59.008 --> 00:25:01.917
Follow the averaged wind, not the strings.

00:25:01.997 --> 00:25:06.330
The paths bend, and drawn against
the clock, they never cross.

00:25:06.410 --> 00:25:11.778
Learn the average exactly, and the
noise lands exactly on the data's crowd.

00:25:12.953 --> 00:25:14.758
Now give the wind a knob.

00:25:14.838 --> 00:25:19.056
Turn it: the target changes
shape, and the cloud follows.

00:25:19.136 --> 00:25:21.202
One network, every setting.

00:25:22.072 --> 00:25:26.614
On the three dials, a real network
trained at the filled dots.

00:25:26.694 --> 00:25:30.714
It was tested at the open dots,
never seen in training.

00:25:30.794 --> 00:25:35.261
In between, no theorem promises
success: only tests can tell.

00:25:35.341 --> 00:25:38.130
Beyond the range, nothing was tested.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:44.372
At an open dot, the network's snapshots
join the shoebox, beside simulated ones.

00:25:45.553 --> 00:25:49.966
A generated snapshot has no
true twin, so we compare crowds.

00:25:50.046 --> 00:25:52.737
Picture each crowd as a pile of sand.

00:25:52.817 --> 00:25:57.455
A distribution distance scores
how different the two piles are.

00:25:57.535 --> 00:26:02.432
The earth-mover kind counts how
much sand must move, and how far.

00:26:03.302 --> 00:26:07.853
A perfect model, scored against
the data: will it get zero?

00:26:11.223 --> 00:26:15.596
No. Roll a fair die a thousand
times, then a thousand more.

00:26:15.676 --> 00:26:19.521
Same die, same truth,
yet the two histograms differ.

00:26:19.601 --> 00:26:24.277
That gap is the noise floor,
about what a perfect model scores.

00:26:24.357 --> 00:26:29.746
Judge every model against it,
at equal sample size, never against zero.

00:26:30.616 --> 00:26:33.703
One catch: the samples
must be independent.

00:26:33.783 --> 00:26:38.454
Overlap them, and the floor drops
too low, as the report warns.

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:42.916
Which statistics must the
generated fields match,

00:26:42.996 --> 00:26:46.736
and how close to the noise
floor counts as success?

00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:55.031
Physics hands the machine
two kinds of laws.

00:26:55.111 --> 00:26:57.642
Some hold for every single snapshot.

00:26:57.722 --> 00:26:59.890
Others hold only for the crowd.

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:01.813
Two shelves, still empty.

00:27:02.683 --> 00:27:07.064
Back to the three dials, at a
Gamma never seen in training.

00:27:07.144 --> 00:27:08.652
Grey: true snapshots.

00:27:08.732 --> 00:27:10.618
Violet: a plain generator.

00:27:10.698 --> 00:27:12.165
The shape is right.

00:27:13.035 --> 00:27:14.499
Now weigh the sides.

00:27:14.579 --> 00:27:17.740
The truth is balanced; the machine is not.

00:27:17.820 --> 00:27:21.522
Only about four in ten of
its snapshots point up.

00:27:21.602 --> 00:27:24.520
It copied its data: below the threshold,

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:29.503
each run stays in one whirlpool,
and the runs split unevenly.

00:27:30.687 --> 00:27:32.544
Two ways to teach the mirror.

00:27:32.624 --> 00:27:34.915
The first student, data augmentation,

00:27:34.995 --> 00:27:38.420
sees each training snapshot
flipped half the time.

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:42.377
The second, equivariance,
has the mirror built into its wind:

00:27:42.457 --> 00:27:46.744
a mirrored noise point always
lands on the mirrored snapshot.

00:27:47.614 --> 00:27:51.343
At a Gamma neither has seen,
who stays balanced?

00:27:55.213 --> 00:27:56.579
The built-in mirror.

00:27:56.659 --> 00:27:58.965
The first student tilts a little.

00:27:59.045 --> 00:28:03.447
Yet in the mirror's crowd,
each snapshot still points one way.

00:28:03.527 --> 00:28:06.990
The mirror balances the
crowd, not each snapshot.

00:28:07.070 --> 00:28:10.859
Its card goes on the crowd
shelf: built in, exactly.

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:14.625
Here is that tilt, setting by setting.

00:28:14.705 --> 00:28:17.846
Below the threshold,
runs often split unevenly:

00:28:17.926 --> 00:28:22.257
at zero point eight six, only
five of sixteen runs point up.

00:28:22.337 --> 00:28:27.666
The plain machine copies the imbalance,
and spills it onto unseen settings.

00:28:28.536 --> 00:28:32.684
Augmentation sits on the noise
floor at its training settings.

00:28:32.764 --> 00:28:36.708
Between them, it drifts:
the network fills the gaps freely.

00:28:36.788 --> 00:28:40.118
The built-in mirror stays
on the floor everywhere.

00:28:40.198 --> 00:28:44.183
Flipped copies teach the mirror
only where there are data.

00:28:45.353 --> 00:28:48.179
Can the energy budget catch the tilt?

00:28:48.259 --> 00:28:51.556
No. Each whirlpool
balances its own budget.

00:28:51.636 --> 00:28:55.169
So a mix of the two passes,
in any proportion.

00:28:55.249 --> 00:29:00.588
On a check of crowd laws, the most
lopsided machine even edges ahead.

00:29:00.668 --> 00:29:03.054
The mirror needs its own tool.

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:06.617
Remember the budget of the shoebox.

00:29:06.697 --> 00:29:09.730
Snapshot by snapshot,
energy rises or falls.

00:29:09.810 --> 00:29:14.935
Only the crowd balances, on average,
like a bank account over the years.

00:29:15.805 --> 00:29:18.169
Now, a tempting fix for the machine.

00:29:18.249 --> 00:29:23.643
Force every generated snapshot to balance
its budget exactly. Better, or worse?

00:29:27.513 --> 00:29:28.013
Worse.

00:29:27.947 --> 00:29:31.438
Push each snapshot until
its energy stops changing:

00:29:31.518 --> 00:29:34.889
the wide pile collapses
into a spike at zero.

00:29:34.969 --> 00:29:37.857
A ledger where every single day balances.

00:29:37.937 --> 00:29:40.283
No real account looks like that.

00:29:41.153 --> 00:29:45.206
The distribution distance
to the truth grows ninefold.

00:29:45.286 --> 00:29:48.833
A short proof says why:
even a perfect generator,

00:29:48.913 --> 00:29:52.094
forced this way, cannot match the truth.

00:29:52.174 --> 00:29:55.002
So the budget goes on the crowd shelf.

00:29:55.082 --> 00:30:00.087
On the next test bench, a cruder
score is fooled by such a spike.

00:30:01.253 --> 00:30:04.536
Impose the budget on the
crowd, during training.

00:30:04.616 --> 00:30:08.601
Draw a batch of snapshots at
any Gamma, even without data.

00:30:08.681 --> 00:30:12.407
Average their energy change
and square it: this penalty,

00:30:12.487 --> 00:30:16.488
the crowd loss, vanishes
only when the crowd balances.

00:30:17.358 --> 00:30:18.643
But there is a trap.

00:30:18.723 --> 00:30:22.117
Even for a balanced crowd,
a batch's average wobbles;

00:30:22.197 --> 00:30:24.646
its square grows with the scatter.

00:30:24.726 --> 00:30:27.144
So training also squeezes the scatter,

00:30:27.224 --> 00:30:31.126
nudging every snapshot toward
balance: toward the spike.

00:30:31.206 --> 00:30:34.238
It showed up while
building this experiment.

00:30:35.108 --> 00:30:38.060
The fix: split the batch
into two independent

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:40.719
juries and multiply their averages.

00:30:40.799 --> 00:30:45.638
On average, the scatter drops out,
leaving only the crowd's imbalance.

00:30:45.718 --> 00:30:48.699
Does it pay off? The next tests will tell.

00:30:50.153 --> 00:30:53.260
In three dimensions,
which budgets should generated

00:30:53.340 --> 00:30:57.169
fields meet on average,
and must their spread match too?

00:31:02.553 --> 00:31:05.705
New test bench: whole
fields, not three dials.

00:31:05.785 --> 00:31:09.992
A flat square of conducting fluid,
its edges wrapping around.

00:31:10.072 --> 00:31:13.100
In blue, the swirl of
the flow; in vermillion,

00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:16.145
the electric currents behind the field.

00:31:17.015 --> 00:31:20.203
One knob, eta: how fast the field smudges.

00:31:20.283 --> 00:31:23.393
But a flat world cannot sustain a dynamo.

00:31:23.473 --> 00:31:26.702
Here a random forcing
keeps the field alive:

00:31:26.782 --> 00:31:30.195
a training ground for laws, not a dynamo.

00:31:31.353 --> 00:31:35.657
Every training field passes the
box test: it is divergence-free.

00:31:35.737 --> 00:31:37.958
Train a plain generator on them.

00:31:38.828 --> 00:31:40.913
Will its fields be divergence-free?

00:31:44.783 --> 00:31:45.283
No.

00:31:45.010 --> 00:31:48.175
Now lines appear and
vanish inside the box.

00:31:48.255 --> 00:31:51.031
Relative to the field's own variations,

00:31:51.111 --> 00:31:55.909
the divergence is zero point
four one: a defect of order one.

00:31:56.779 --> 00:32:00.508
A soft penalty only lowers
it to zero point three

00:32:00.588 --> 00:32:04.850
four for the flow, zero
point one six for the field.

00:32:04.930 --> 00:32:10.044
And the magnetic energy comes out
two point seven times too high.

00:32:10.124 --> 00:32:14.069
This law belongs on the
shelf for every snapshot.

00:32:15.253 --> 00:32:18.079
First fix: draw a hill map, not arrows,

00:32:18.159 --> 00:32:21.733
and let the flow run
along its contour lines.

00:32:21.813 --> 00:32:24.573
Where the lines crowd, it runs faster,

00:32:24.653 --> 00:32:28.833
so nothing piles up or leaks,
whatever the landscape.

00:32:28.913 --> 00:32:31.721
The box agrees: lines in, lines out.

00:32:32.591 --> 00:32:36.952
So the machine draws two hill maps,
for the flow and the field:

00:32:37.032 --> 00:32:41.662
zero divergence by construction,
however badly it is trained.

00:32:42.532 --> 00:32:48.532
Second fix, for any field: split it into
swirl plus slope, and delete the slope.

00:32:48.684 --> 00:32:54.480
Wave by wave, remove the part of each
arrow along the wave's own direction.

00:32:55.350 --> 00:33:00.360
The gauges fall to rounding error,
down to ten to the minus seventeen.

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:04.832
Measured, though, with the very
operator used to enforce it.

00:33:05.987 --> 00:33:11.407
Back to the plain generator:
why does it miss, trained on clean data?

00:33:11.487 --> 00:33:14.914
Follow the part of a
field breaking the law.

00:33:14.994 --> 00:33:19.429
To bring it exactly to zero,
the wind must act as a spring

00:33:19.509 --> 00:33:24.510
that turns infinitely stiff as
the transport clock runs out.

00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:31.089
A bounded network cannot, and a sampler
with finite steps cannot follow either.

00:33:31.169 --> 00:33:34.862
A gap of order one remains:
almost is not exactly.

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:40.619
Now the crowd law: on average,
energy in equals energy out.

00:33:40.699 --> 00:33:45.091
Three ways to impose it,
at settings of eta never seen.

00:33:45.961 --> 00:33:49.893
One: during training, the crowd
loss, with its two juries.

00:33:49.973 --> 00:33:52.442
Against a control run of equal length,

00:33:52.522 --> 00:33:57.571
the distribution distance drops from
two point five to zero point seven.

00:33:57.651 --> 00:33:59.964
The control itself gains nothing.

00:34:00.834 --> 00:34:04.388
Two: after training,
reweight the generated crowd.

00:34:04.468 --> 00:34:07.764
Where the model is worst,
a poll of five hundred

00:34:07.844 --> 00:34:11.549
shrinks to one person,
and the crowd gets worse.

00:34:12.419 --> 00:34:15.546
Three: rescale every
snapshot onto the budget.

00:34:15.626 --> 00:34:18.696
Each snapshot now dissipates
the same: a spike,

00:34:18.776 --> 00:34:21.543
under a thousandth of the true spread.

00:34:21.623 --> 00:34:26.912
Yet the same distribution distance,
built on spectra, improves: it is fooled.

00:34:26.992 --> 00:34:31.764
Only the spread gives the spike away.
The crowd loss shows no spike.

00:34:32.634 --> 00:34:35.231
One training seed each,
interpolation only,

00:34:35.311 --> 00:34:38.006
every model far above the noise floor.

00:34:39.187 --> 00:34:41.584
Back to the two shelves, now full.

00:34:41.664 --> 00:34:45.050
For every snapshot: zero
divergence. Build it in,

00:34:45.130 --> 00:34:49.491
or project, and it holds exactly;
a penalty only roughly.

00:34:50.361 --> 00:34:54.108
For the crowd: the energy budget.
Impose it on the crowd

00:34:54.188 --> 00:34:58.892
during training. Reweighting afterwards
fails where the model is poor;

00:34:58.972 --> 00:35:02.309
forcing it onto each
snapshot makes a spike.

00:35:03.179 --> 00:35:08.231
And the mirror? Each snapshot is
lopsided, so it is a crowd law,

00:35:08.311 --> 00:35:12.250
yet a network can carry
it exactly, built in.

00:35:13.120 --> 00:35:18.081
One rule sorts them all:
enforce each law where it is true.

00:35:21.153 --> 00:35:25.246
Put the pieces together:
the machine this study proposes.

00:35:25.326 --> 00:35:30.224
A wind carries noise to a symmetric
crowd of codes, mirror built in.

00:35:30.304 --> 00:35:35.788
A decoder turns each code into hill maps,
so every field is divergence-free.

00:35:35.868 --> 00:35:39.265
And the energy budget
is imposed on the crowd.

00:35:40.135 --> 00:35:44.736
It has not been built as a whole:
the experiments tested pieces,

00:35:44.816 --> 00:35:50.816
on three dials and a flat sheet, with
small networks on an ordinary processor.

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:56.682
One question remains: from snapshots,
how well can we answer how often?

00:35:57.953 --> 00:36:01.332
In the partners' solver snapshots,
which laws hold exactly,

00:36:01.412 --> 00:36:03.736
and under which discrete operator?

00:36:08.920 --> 00:36:12.589
The toll counter is back:
every car is now generated.

00:36:12.669 --> 00:36:16.204
They come from the machine
with the built-in mirror:

00:36:16.284 --> 00:36:18.707
twenty thousand at each of forty-one

00:36:18.787 --> 00:36:21.997
settings of Gamma,
most unseen in training.

00:36:22.077 --> 00:36:26.210
Rice, with the equations,
turns them into a crossing rate.

00:36:27.080 --> 00:36:29.943
Against the counted truth,
far from the threshold,

00:36:30.023 --> 00:36:32.657
the cumulative error is fourteen percent.

00:36:32.737 --> 00:36:35.970
Setting by setting, the miss
averages seventeen percent,

00:36:36.050 --> 00:36:39.051
and reaches sixty-five
percent at two settings.

00:36:39.131 --> 00:36:42.727
Near the threshold, the error
climbs to forty-two percent.

00:36:43.597 --> 00:36:45.661
One model, one training seed.

00:36:45.741 --> 00:36:50.245
Rice needs the equations,
and gives a rate, not waiting times.

00:36:50.325 --> 00:36:54.977
So, the study's third question:
can a machine tell us how often?

00:36:55.057 --> 00:37:01.057
For crossings on three dials: far from the
threshold, roughly yes; near it, not yet.

00:37:02.853 --> 00:37:07.146
Zoom in. The truth is silent
up to zero point eight six.

00:37:07.226 --> 00:37:10.691
But the machine changes
smoothly with Gamma.

00:37:11.561 --> 00:37:16.112
Will its crossing rate switch
on early, late, or on time?

00:37:19.982 --> 00:37:22.504
Early and late, but never on time.

00:37:22.584 --> 00:37:25.900
Below the threshold,
it leaks false crossings:

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:28.932
up to a tenth of the rate just above.

00:37:29.012 --> 00:37:35.012
At zero point eight six five, it is late:
less than a sixth of the counted rate.

00:37:35.133 --> 00:37:38.386
Just above, it overshoots:
five crossings per

00:37:38.466 --> 00:37:42.204
thousand time units,
against three point four.

00:37:43.387 --> 00:37:46.394
Why? Picture the machine as a stiff sheet:

00:37:46.474 --> 00:37:50.148
as Gamma turns, its crowd
changes only so fast.

00:37:50.228 --> 00:37:56.228
The truth climbs a steep ramp: just above
zero point eight six, the corridor opens.

00:37:57.438 --> 00:38:01.452
The sheet rounds the corner:
too early, then too low.

00:38:01.532 --> 00:38:07.532
The data show both: early strays in the
corridor, then too few at the threshold.

00:38:08.612 --> 00:38:14.612
A theorem: at a true jump, a smooth
machine misses by at least half of it.

00:38:15.049 --> 00:38:20.559
Unless it is very supple,
or told which state the system is in.

00:38:21.429 --> 00:38:27.204
Here, a ramp, not a jump, and one seed:
consistent with the theorem, not proof.

00:38:27.284 --> 00:38:30.501
Nor does the theorem
explain the overshoot.

00:38:32.087 --> 00:38:35.818
The crowd loss, on three
dials, at unseen settings.

00:38:35.898 --> 00:38:39.785
On the good machine,
it halves the crowd-law residuals,

00:38:39.865 --> 00:38:42.393
even for laws it never enforced.

00:38:42.473 --> 00:38:48.473
But the spread of energy changes shrinks
by a quarter: a faint echo of the spike.

00:38:49.425 --> 00:38:52.288
On a deliberately biased
machine, it fails:

00:38:52.368 --> 00:38:55.189
one run diverged, the other got worse.

00:38:55.269 --> 00:38:57.663
On the crossing rate, with one seed:

00:38:57.743 --> 00:39:01.784
slightly better near the
threshold, worse far from it.

00:39:03.353 --> 00:39:06.311
Now the strictest judge: the noise floor.

00:39:06.391 --> 00:39:09.605
On three dials, the best
machine sits at about

00:39:09.685 --> 00:39:13.055
twice its floor;
but three numbers are easy.

00:39:13.135 --> 00:39:17.019
On the flat sheet of plasma,
every model's spectra miss

00:39:17.099 --> 00:39:21.465
far more than two data samples
differ: at best, sixfold.

00:39:22.335 --> 00:39:28.335
Two seeds on the dials, one on the sheet;
the PhD plan asks for at least three.

00:39:28.558 --> 00:39:31.817
Every test sits between trained settings.

00:39:33.387 --> 00:39:36.790
The scoreboard: eight mechanisms,
each with a verdict.

00:39:36.870 --> 00:39:39.555
For every snapshot:
hill maps and projection,

00:39:39.635 --> 00:39:41.821
exact; the penalty, approximate.

00:39:41.901 --> 00:39:44.854
For the crowd: the mirror,
exact; the crowd loss,

00:39:44.934 --> 00:39:47.801
useful but not clean; the rest, failures.

00:39:48.671 --> 00:39:51.222
Back to the study's three questions.

00:39:51.302 --> 00:39:54.674
One: between the dots,
the shapes come out right,

00:39:54.754 --> 00:39:57.581
but no model reaches its noise floor.

00:39:57.661 --> 00:40:02.040
Two: each law where it holds,
exactly yes, on average partly.

00:40:02.120 --> 00:40:07.194
Three, how often: roughly right far
from the threshold, poor near it.

00:40:08.064 --> 00:40:08.983
Three lessons.

00:40:09.063 --> 00:40:10.838
Exact laws: build them in.

00:40:10.918 --> 00:40:15.191
Crowd laws: impose them on the
crowd, never on each snapshot.

00:40:15.271 --> 00:40:20.012
And judge every result against the
noise floor, with enough seeds.

00:40:21.987 --> 00:40:26.181
Which settings should get runs
long enough to count reversals,

00:40:26.261 --> 00:40:29.003
so a predicted rate can be checked?

00:40:34.187 --> 00:40:37.468
So far, every test ran
on toy models. Before

00:40:37.548 --> 00:40:41.052
real dynamos, back to
the map of the field.

00:40:41.132 --> 00:40:46.135
Zoom into its thin data-driven
lane: it opens into four tracks.

00:40:47.005 --> 00:40:51.339
A laboratory dynamo sets every
track four tests: three dimensions,

00:40:51.419 --> 00:40:56.898
walls with iron and a vacuum, a tiny
magnetic Prandtl number, and rare flips.

00:40:58.453 --> 00:41:02.635
First track: fast surrogates,
networks trained to imitate a solver.

00:41:02.715 --> 00:41:07.661
Some carry the equations in their loss;
others learn maps between whole fields.

00:41:07.741 --> 00:41:12.178
For conducting fluids, they now reach
flat, two-dimensional turbulence.

00:41:12.258 --> 00:41:15.246
For the Earth, recall the
twenty twenty-two study:

00:41:15.326 --> 00:41:19.031
the methods it tried could
not reliably foresee a flip.

00:41:19.901 --> 00:41:23.670
Their weak spot: trained to
minimize a mean-square error,

00:41:23.750 --> 00:41:26.874
ensembles of them
regress to the mean flow.

00:41:26.954 --> 00:41:30.616
A cloud with two polarities
collapses to its middle.

00:41:31.787 --> 00:41:33.464
Second track: generative models,

00:41:33.544 --> 00:41:36.455
which learn the whole cloud,
not just its middle.

00:41:36.535 --> 00:41:38.828
Adversarial networks
first, then diffusion,

00:41:38.908 --> 00:41:41.666
then flow matching,
the family the study used.

00:41:41.746 --> 00:41:45.575
They now dream three-dimensional
turbulence, global weather ensembles,

00:41:45.655 --> 00:41:48.666
and steady turbulent
states for chosen settings.

00:41:49.536 --> 00:41:52.443
Trained on dynamo
simulations? We found none.

00:41:54.420 --> 00:41:58.254
Third track: making samples
obey the laws. Four families.

00:41:58.334 --> 00:42:02.528
Hard: build the law into the network,
say a potential whose curl

00:42:02.608 --> 00:42:06.769
has no divergence, or project
each sample while drawing it.

00:42:06.849 --> 00:42:10.545
Soft: a penalty in the loss,
obeyed only approximately.

00:42:10.625 --> 00:42:13.266
On the distribution: constrain the crowd,

00:42:13.346 --> 00:42:17.481
by its covariance, or by the
equation a steady cloud obeys.

00:42:17.561 --> 00:42:21.573
And symmetry: an equivariant
flow gives a symmetric cloud.

00:42:22.843 --> 00:42:26.060
A twenty twenty-six preprint
compared ways to impose zero

00:42:26.140 --> 00:42:29.542
divergence on flat flows:
no single strategy dominates.

00:42:29.622 --> 00:42:33.600
In three dimensions, we found two
exactly divergence-free generators,

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:35.952
both preprints: periodic-box turbulence,

00:42:36.032 --> 00:42:38.899
and a current-free field
in the Sun's corona.

00:42:38.979 --> 00:42:42.188
None handles walls, a vacuum
outside and iron inside.

00:42:43.353 --> 00:42:47.760
Fourth track: data assimilation,
merging a model with a few measurements.

00:42:47.840 --> 00:42:52.616
Learned priors came in through compressed
latent spaces, then diffusion models.

00:42:52.696 --> 00:42:57.767
And a library now plugs deep networks into
Kalman filters and variational methods.

00:42:58.637 --> 00:43:03.381
We found no diffusion prior used
this way for conducting fluids.

00:43:03.461 --> 00:43:06.530
Near a flip, the answer
has two polarities,

00:43:06.610 --> 00:43:11.406
and a prior that ignores the
mirror could bias it toward one.

00:43:12.587 --> 00:43:15.623
Now the rings: papers
co-signed by the supervisors.

00:43:15.703 --> 00:43:20.077
Dynamo side: Taylor–Green dynamos pushed
to small magnetic Prandtl numbers,

00:43:20.157 --> 00:43:24.238
and the VKS tank with its iron
impellers, simulated in two codes.

00:43:24.318 --> 00:43:27.537
In twenty twenty-six, both
codes produced VKS reversals,

00:43:27.617 --> 00:43:30.959
and a four-dial model
reproduces their main features.

00:43:31.039 --> 00:43:34.071
Learning side: autoencoder
latent spaces read through

00:43:34.151 --> 00:43:37.130
classical flow modes,
von Kármán flow included.

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:39.639
And assimilation and
diffusion models built

00:43:39.719 --> 00:43:42.617
on deep networks,
outside conducting fluids.

00:43:43.887 --> 00:43:47.614
Three ingredients are in hand.
But in what we found,

00:43:47.694 --> 00:43:53.369
none of the four has published a
generative model trained on dynamo data.

00:43:54.553 --> 00:43:57.530
Where the generative and
constraint tracks cross

00:43:57.610 --> 00:44:00.505
the dynamo tracks, we found the map empty.

00:44:00.585 --> 00:44:03.631
An independent thesis
proposal aims at that spot:

00:44:03.711 --> 00:44:06.912
five gaps, each beside
its nearest neighbour.

00:44:07.782 --> 00:44:09.985
One: a generator of dynamo fields.

00:44:10.065 --> 00:44:12.906
Two: velocity and magnetic
field with exactly

00:44:12.986 --> 00:44:17.035
zero divergence, in a walled
cylinder with iron and vacuum.

00:44:17.115 --> 00:44:20.277
Three: energy budgets
imposed on the distribution,

00:44:20.357 --> 00:44:23.883
with no target value,
even where there are no data.

00:44:23.963 --> 00:44:27.912
Four: the flip rate read from
snapshots with Rice's formula.

00:44:27.992 --> 00:44:31.647
Five: one generator that
crosses the dynamo threshold.

00:44:32.917 --> 00:44:37.760
The study tested pieces on toy cases:
exact potentials on a flat sheet,

00:44:37.840 --> 00:44:42.399
a budget imposed without data,
Rice's formula on three dials.

00:44:43.553 --> 00:44:48.366
The field moves fast: a twenty twenty-six
conference paper claims to be the

00:44:48.446 --> 00:44:53.624
first to recognize a trade-off between
physical and distributional accuracy.

00:44:53.704 --> 00:44:56.380
The study's lesson,
never force a crowd law

00:44:56.460 --> 00:44:59.610
on each snapshot,
must be placed against it.

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:03.074
Not claimed: waiting times between flips,

00:45:03.154 --> 00:45:08.462
sodium's tiny magnetic Prandtl number,
the Earth's core, real laboratory data.

00:45:08.542 --> 00:45:11.558
Each empty spot is a
search result, not a proof,

00:45:11.638 --> 00:45:14.483
to be checked again before every paper.

00:45:16.153 --> 00:45:19.416
First VKS data: should
the generator train on the

00:45:19.496 --> 00:45:23.169
LATU periodic grid,
or the SFEMaNS cylinder mesh?

00:45:28.353 --> 00:45:29.928
Back to the staircase.

00:45:30.008 --> 00:45:33.479
The first two steps are
lit by small experiments:

00:45:33.559 --> 00:45:36.170
three numbers, then a flat sheet.

00:45:36.250 --> 00:45:39.890
The third, the twisting
box, has only a preview.

00:45:40.760 --> 00:45:43.256
From there up, every step is a bet,

00:45:43.336 --> 00:45:48.598
written down before the data exist,
in an independent PhD proposal.

00:45:49.753 --> 00:45:54.020
To climb, the study first
built a factory for the data.

00:45:54.100 --> 00:45:56.312
A recipe card sets the knobs.

00:45:56.392 --> 00:46:01.290
Ovens run one simulation per setting,
on a laptop or a cluster.

00:46:01.370 --> 00:46:04.349
Each result is sealed in a labelled jar:

00:46:04.429 --> 00:46:08.276
a unique number,
the code version, a checksum.

00:46:09.146 --> 00:46:14.311
A ledger records every jar, a menu
splits them for training and testing,

00:46:14.391 --> 00:46:18.217
the chef learns, and a
critic scores the result.

00:46:19.087 --> 00:46:22.952
It all runs end to end
on an ordinary processor,

00:46:23.032 --> 00:46:27.114
never yet on a graphics
card or a real cluster.

00:46:27.194 --> 00:46:31.446
A grey tag names the file
each empty frame awaits.

00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:35.277
First product off the
line: the flat sheet,

00:46:35.357 --> 00:46:38.124
eleven diffusivities, sixteen runs each.

00:46:38.204 --> 00:46:41.156
The bet, a check on the
data: at every setting,

00:46:41.236 --> 00:46:46.212
the average dissipation matches the
injected power, within five percent.

00:46:47.587 --> 00:46:49.299
Bet one: the twisting box.

00:46:49.379 --> 00:46:53.801
At thirty-two cubed, the small
sweep matched the published windows,

00:46:53.881 --> 00:46:58.713
but near seventeen and a half the
sign of growth stayed unresolved.

00:46:59.583 --> 00:47:05.160
The campaign doubles the resolution and
steps Rm by whole numbers up to forty.

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:08.308
The bet: growth still
positive at seventeen,

00:47:08.388 --> 00:47:12.099
already negative at eighteen,
with two fits agreeing:

00:47:12.179 --> 00:47:16.873
one over the kept part of the run,
one over its last quarter.

00:47:18.543 --> 00:47:21.859
Confidence: medium. At eighteen,
the small sweep's two

00:47:21.939 --> 00:47:25.366
fits already differed by
more than the rate itself.

00:47:25.446 --> 00:47:30.491
Worth asking before the run: add three
points between seventeen and eighteen?

00:47:31.853 --> 00:47:33.926
Bet two, the first true dynamo:

00:47:34.006 --> 00:47:37.491
a Taylor-Green flow of
counter-rotating eddies.

00:47:37.571 --> 00:47:40.242
Now the field pushes back on the flow.

00:47:40.322 --> 00:47:44.623
Above a threshold, a tiny seed
should grow, then level off.

00:47:45.493 --> 00:47:50.394
Twelve runs cross four Reynolds
numbers with three values of Pm.

00:47:50.474 --> 00:47:55.797
The bet: a threshold in Rm,
bracketed for at least two values of Pm.

00:47:57.467 --> 00:48:02.217
Confidence: medium. Published runs
define the Reynolds number differently,

00:48:02.297 --> 00:48:05.128
so the threshold may fall off this grid.

00:48:05.208 --> 00:48:09.907
Near threshold, a run may still be
growing when its snapshots are kept.

00:48:09.987 --> 00:48:13.113
Lengthen those runs,
or flag and exclude them?

00:48:14.487 --> 00:48:16.004
Some things need no bet.

00:48:16.084 --> 00:48:19.146
In three dimensions,
hill maps become potentials:

00:48:19.226 --> 00:48:22.524
the generator draws them,
then takes their curl.

00:48:22.604 --> 00:48:26.292
So the flow and the field stay
divergence-free to rounding,

00:48:26.372 --> 00:48:28.490
even with untrained weights.

00:48:29.360 --> 00:48:34.548
Mirror the starting noise, and the
sample comes out with its field flipped.

00:48:34.628 --> 00:48:37.609
That shows the mechanism, not the quality.

00:48:38.787 --> 00:48:41.116
Everything else must be measured.

00:48:41.196 --> 00:48:45.381
First test: does the generator
get the energy spectra right,

00:48:45.461 --> 00:48:49.913
within a tenth of a decade,
at the settings it trained on?

00:48:50.783 --> 00:48:51.821
Confidence: low.

00:48:51.901 --> 00:48:55.568
In two dimensions, the best
models only just got there,

00:48:55.648 --> 00:49:01.087
and here each run keeps forty snapshots,
against five thousand in the plan.

00:49:03.153 --> 00:49:07.937
Leave the periodic box for a real
vessel, and exact gets harder.

00:49:08.017 --> 00:49:11.525
The potential needs a
gauge and a wall condition;

00:49:11.605 --> 00:49:14.279
then each field has exactly one.

00:49:15.149 --> 00:49:20.343
At the soft-iron impellers, field lines
bend sharply at the iron's surface,

00:49:20.423 --> 00:49:25.042
so a smooth potential cannot be
exact: it needs a kink there.

00:49:26.412 --> 00:49:31.221
Outside, the field must match a
current-free field in the vacuum.

00:49:31.301 --> 00:49:33.551
The rule is linear but nonlocal:

00:49:33.631 --> 00:49:38.292
the field at one point of the
wall depends on the whole wall.

00:49:38.372 --> 00:49:43.291
Imposing it exactly and cheaply,
for every sample, is still open.

00:49:44.453 --> 00:49:47.322
The top steps are
simulations of real machines:

00:49:47.402 --> 00:49:51.058
the VKS cylinder, and DRESDYN,
a precessing cylinder.

00:49:51.928 --> 00:49:53.917
Their bets are already written.

00:49:53.997 --> 00:49:57.875
For VKS: the four main patterns
with the right energy shares,

00:49:57.955 --> 00:50:02.393
and Rice's reversal rate inside
the simulated count's error bars.

00:50:02.473 --> 00:50:07.575
For DRESDYN: the threshold at an unseen
precession rate, within ten percent.

00:50:08.945 --> 00:50:11.990
Those simulations belong
to the project's partners,

00:50:12.070 --> 00:50:14.728
are not public, and must be requested.

00:50:16.387 --> 00:50:19.958
Before the Taylor-Green runs, which
forcing, Reynolds number definition

00:50:20.038 --> 00:50:23.814
and resolution make their threshold
comparable with published ones?

00:50:28.987 --> 00:50:32.454
Every chapter so far
has ended on a question.

00:50:32.534 --> 00:50:34.770
The empty seats raised more.

00:50:35.640 --> 00:50:38.437
Hang each one under the step it shapes.

00:50:38.517 --> 00:50:43.158
Bet one: whether to add settings
between seventeen and eighteen.

00:50:43.238 --> 00:50:47.805
Bet two: the seed, the Reynolds
number, the length of each run.

00:50:47.885 --> 00:50:50.313
The generator: what it must match.

00:50:50.393 --> 00:50:52.931
The top steps: the partners' data.

00:50:53.801 --> 00:50:57.410
A staircase of bets is a
staircase of decisions,

00:50:57.490 --> 00:51:00.817
and the first ones come before any run.

00:51:02.787 --> 00:51:06.516
First, the runs: these answers
change what gets computed.

00:51:07.386 --> 00:51:11.655
Near a threshold, start from a
tiny seed, a strong one, or both?

00:51:11.735 --> 00:51:15.121
Which values of Pm, since
sodium's is out of reach?

00:51:15.201 --> 00:51:18.791
Settings spread evenly,
or packed near each threshold?

00:51:18.871 --> 00:51:21.714
How many independent
snapshots per setting?

00:51:21.794 --> 00:51:24.500
How long must runs near a threshold last?

00:51:24.580 --> 00:51:27.653
Which forcing, Reynolds
number and grid make the

00:51:27.733 --> 00:51:31.500
Taylor-Green threshold
comparable with published ones?

00:51:31.580 --> 00:51:33.717
And which campaign comes first?

00:51:34.887 --> 00:51:40.575
Then the framing: how each result will
be judged, decided before the data exist.

00:51:40.655 --> 00:51:45.223
Which statistics must generated
fields match, down to the shape of

00:51:45.303 --> 00:51:50.452
their field lines, and how close to
the noise floor counts as success?

00:51:50.532 --> 00:51:55.465
Which budgets must hold on average,
and must their spread match too?

00:51:56.620 --> 00:52:00.920
One answer, not about physics,
could make most renting unnecessary.

00:52:01.000 --> 00:52:02.403
Which question is it?

00:52:05.773 --> 00:52:07.876
Where the computing happens.

00:52:07.956 --> 00:52:12.904
If a lab has graphics cards an
intern can use, or there is already

00:52:12.984 --> 00:52:17.852
a Jean Zay project to join,
renting shrinks to a short trial.

00:52:17.932 --> 00:52:23.886
And for the top steps: which partner
simulations, in what form, and by when?

00:52:25.053 --> 00:52:28.196
The second shelf matters
for the internship plan

00:52:28.276 --> 00:52:31.854
and the data request,
not for the first campaigns.

00:52:31.934 --> 00:52:35.676
Which settings get runs long
enough to count reversals?

00:52:35.756 --> 00:52:38.456
Which operator decides what exact means?

00:52:38.536 --> 00:52:41.097
Which labels must each snapshot carry?

00:52:41.177 --> 00:52:43.987
And may new results appear in this film?

00:52:44.857 --> 00:52:50.857
The last shelf can wait: the top steps'
details, and how to frame a first paper.

00:52:52.620 --> 00:52:57.579
The first shelf fits in one message,
to write before renting anything.

00:52:57.659 --> 00:52:59.883
This ranking is only a proposal.

00:52:59.963 --> 00:53:02.513
The supervisors may well change it.

00:53:03.383 --> 00:53:06.360
Several answers could
change what gets computed,

00:53:06.440 --> 00:53:09.017
or whether anything is rented at all.

00:53:09.097 --> 00:53:11.749
The cheapest experiment
is a good question,

00:53:11.829 --> 00:53:14.509
asked before renting a graphics card.

00:53:20.087 --> 00:53:22.158
Questions first, then computing.

00:53:22.238 --> 00:53:25.721
Here is what those questions
rest on, in two columns.

00:53:25.801 --> 00:53:30.829
On the left, what three small experiments
measured on an ordinary processor.

00:53:30.909 --> 00:53:33.431
Toy problems, one or two training seeds,

00:53:33.511 --> 00:53:38.199
and machines tested only between
the settings they were trained on.

00:53:39.069 --> 00:53:45.069
On the right, what the staircase bets,
each with the rule that will settle it.

00:53:45.328 --> 00:53:49.761
Each lamp shows a verdict only
once its data file arrives,

00:53:49.841 --> 00:53:52.661
and the film is updated with it.

00:53:54.420 --> 00:53:58.149
Back to the compass,
and to the two questions.

00:53:59.519 --> 00:54:01.421
When will this needle turn?

00:54:01.501 --> 00:54:03.624
For the Earth, no one can say.

00:54:03.704 --> 00:54:06.668
In a simple computer
model, chaos makes the

00:54:06.748 --> 00:54:10.012
forecast fade before
the average wait ends.

00:54:10.092 --> 00:54:16.092
But how often the field flips, counted as
crossings of zero, can still be answered.

00:54:16.186 --> 00:54:21.099
The answer hides in a shoebox of
snapshots, read with the equations.

00:54:21.179 --> 00:54:27.083
And a machine can learn to dream such
snapshots, between the simulated settings.

00:54:28.353 --> 00:54:31.221
On small test problems, it partly works.

00:54:31.301 --> 00:54:34.979
The mirror holds by construction,
at every setting.

00:54:35.059 --> 00:54:37.979
On three dials, the rate of zero crossings

00:54:38.059 --> 00:54:42.423
is roughly right far from the
threshold, and poor near it.

00:54:42.503 --> 00:54:45.592
A smooth machine rounds
off a steep change.

00:54:45.672 --> 00:54:49.012
The bigger tests,
in three dimensions and in the

00:54:49.092 --> 00:54:52.629
shapes of real machines,
are written as bets.

00:54:54.099 --> 00:54:55.480
One lesson remains.

00:54:55.560 --> 00:54:58.092
Enforce each law where it is true.

00:54:58.172 --> 00:55:00.167
Exactly, where it is exact.

00:55:00.247 --> 00:55:03.582
On average, where it
only holds on average.

00:55:07.353 --> 00:55:11.424
The report, the code,
and every result in this film are

00:55:11.504 --> 00:55:15.725
on the project's site,
with their limits written out.

00:55:15.805 --> 00:55:19.712
The rest of the staircase
is still to be computed.
